WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Security continues after release because software risk changes over time.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:15.973
New vulnerabilities are disclosed, dependencies age, configurations drift, business requirements change, and operational incidents reveal assumptions that did not hold.

3
00:00:15.973 --> 00:00:20.923
Logging and monitoring help teams see whether the system behaves as expected.

4
00:00:20.923 --> 00:00:25.797
Incident response readiness helps them act quickly when something is wrong.

5
00:00:25.797 --> 00:00:30.072
Vulnerability disclosure intake and bug reports need a clear path.

6
00:00:30.072 --> 00:00:39.146
People inside and outside the organization may report weaknesses, and those reports should be acknowledged, triaged, assigned, and tracked.

7
00:00:39.146 --> 00:00:47.095
Patch planning should consider severity, exposure, customer impact, exploitability at a high level, and operational risk.

8
00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:53.244
Remediation validation confirms that the fix worked and did not simply move the issue elsewhere.

9
00:00:53.244 --> 00:00:57.144
Operations feedback strengthens earlier lifecycle stages.

10
00:00:57.144 --> 00:01:04.418
If incidents repeatedly involve missing logs, future requirements should include better logging expectations.

11
00:01:04.418 --> 00:01:09.893
If dependency updates are painful, build and testing practices may need improvement.

12
00:01:09.893 --> 00:01:16.942
If configuration drift causes production differences, infrastructure and release controls may need review.

13
00:01:16.942 --> 00:01:21.067
Secure SDLC is a loop, not a straight line that ends at deployment.

14
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:26.766
Metrics and lessons learned help teams improve without turning security into paperwork.

15
00:01:26.766 --> 00:01:40.190
Useful measures might include time to remediate critical findings, percentage of services with threat models, dependency freshness, repeat vulnerability rates, security test coverage, and incident themes.

16
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:47.539
The goal is evidence-backed improvement: learn from real work, adjust standards, and reduce risk in the next cycle.
